==English==

===Etymology===
{{etyl|enm|en}} {{term|lang=enm|duweliche||rightly, properly}}, from {{term|lang=enm|dewe||due}} + {{term|lang=enm|-liche||-ly}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en-us-duly.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|uːli|lang=en}}

===Adverb===
{{en-adv}}

# In a [[due]], [[fit]], or [[becoming]] manner; as it ought to be; [[properly]].
#: ''The citizen's concern was '''duly''' noted in the meeting minutes.''
#* {{quote-news
|year=2011
|date=October 29
|author=Phil McNulty
|title=Chelsea   3 - 5   Arsenal
|work=BBC Sport
|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/15413593.stm
|page=
|passage=Walcott's display deserved a goal and it '''duly''' arrived after 55 minutes. As he had done throughout, the forward ran straight at Chelsea's defence, riding two challenges and even falling before firing an emphatic shot past Cech.}}
# [[regularly|Regularly]]; at the proper [[time]].
#* {{quote-book|year=1907|author=[[w:Harold Edward Bindloss|Harold Bindloss]]
|title=The Dust of Conflict
|chapter=4|url=http://openlibrary.org/works/OL4429277W
|passage=The inquest on keeper Davidson was '''duly''' held, and at the commencement seemed likely to cause Tony Palliser less anxiety than he had expected.}}

